# {{ ansible_managed }}
[Unit]
Description=Start a SMART extended self-test on the homelab data disk

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# LONG, and the short test this replaced is the reason #207 went unseen for a
# week. Both halves of that are measured.
#
# --- What the short test cannot do -------------------------------------------
# `-t short` samples: electronics, a read of the first and last few megabytes,
# a seek test. It does not walk the surface. It could neither have found the
# three bad regions on this drive nor tracked them, and it reported "Completed
# without error" every Wednesday while one of them was making a live Nextcloud
# file unreadable. Same shape as the timer masked in base/filesystem-checks.yml
# and as the offsite CPU-temperature gap in #201 — a control that reports on
# something it does not exercise.
#
# --- The 2026-08-15 measurement that removed it, and why it was wrong --------
# This unit used to carry a careful argument for `-t short`: the long test
# returned "Completed: read failure" at LBA 730424728 within seconds, twice at
# the identical LBA, while that LBA and the 64 MiB around it read WITHOUT ERROR
# from the host, with every SMART counter at zero and nothing in dmesg. Read as
# a USB-native SMR bridge returning a canned result rather than a finding.
#
# Every one of those observations was correct. The conclusion drawn from them
# was not. That is exactly what a marginal sector looks like: the host path
# succeeds through retries and ECC, the self-test reads strictly and fails, and
# the pending counter cannot clear because only a WRITE retires a pending
# sector. The evidence used to dismiss the result was the signature of the
# defect being real.
#
# What it cost: the extended test was the ONLY control on this host that read
# the live surface. Removing it left the nightly restic backup, which skips a
# file whose metadata has not changed and therefore cannot see rot in a file
# that never changes; `restic check --read-data-subset`, which reads the
# repository and not the live tree; and the daily superblock read, which
# reports errors the kernel has already noticed. None of them read cold bytes.
# Nine days later a second sector was throwing unrecoverable read errors into
# the kernel log and a third had taken a live file with it.
#
# --- What was measured on 2026-08-25, after repairing the three regions ------
# All three were rewritten in place (`dd conv=notrunc` onto the same extents,
# verified by filefrag), which is what forces the drive to rewrite or reallocate
# a pending sector. Raw `O_DIRECT` reads of LBA 730424728, 7131556272 and
# 9320359320 then all succeeded, including the one that had been a hard
# Unrecovered read error minutes earlier. `Reallocated_Sector_Ct` stayed 0: the
# drive rewrote in place, it did not need to retire anything.
#
# With those repaired, the extended test ran past the point where it had
# previously aborted three times in a row within seconds — which settles the
# bridge question that could not be settled in August: the drive implements it.
#
# --- Cost ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# 228 minutes announced, longer in practice because the drive suspends the scan
# while the host does I/O. It is a background test: no host I/O of its own, no
# volume to unmount, nothing stopped. That is the property that made it the
# right answer here — the surface question does NOT need the maintenance window
# an `e2fsck -c` would need.
#
# --- Overlap is not a failure, and the short test could never meet it ---------
# Found by this change on 2026-08-26, within a minute of deploying it. Moving
# the timer from 04:30 to 07:30 made systemd treat the just-passed Wednesday
# 07:30 as a missed run — `Persistent=true` doing exactly its job — so the
# service fired immediately, onto a drive already running a manual extended
# test. smartctl refused, correctly:
#
#   Can't start self-test without aborting current test (10% remaining)
#   exit status 4
#
# systemd recorded a FAILED unit, and `systemctl --failed` staying empty is one
# of this lab's own assertions — so a benign overlap turned the posture spec red.
#
# A two-minute short test could not collide with anything. A four-hour one can:
# with a manual run, or a scheduled run that has not finished when the next
# Wednesday comes round, this is reachable in normal operation.
#
# ExecCondition rather than a wrapper or SuccessExitStatus=4, and the difference
# matters. A non-zero ExecCondition SKIPS the unit without marking it failed,
# which is what "a test is already running, there is nothing to do" actually
# means. SuccessExitStatus=4 would swallow the other things smartctl reports
# with that bit — a failed command to the disk, a checksum error — which are
# precisely what this unit exists to surface.
#
# The condition is inverted on purpose: it exits 0 when NO test is in progress.
# If smartctl itself cannot talk to the drive it prints no match, the condition
# passes, and ExecStart runs and fails loudly. The overlap case is silenced; the
# broken case is not.
ExecCondition=/bin/sh -c "! /usr/sbin/smartctl -c {{ data_disk_device | regex_replace('[0-9]+$', '') }} | grep -q 'Self-test routine in progress'"
ExecStart=/usr/sbin/smartctl -t long {{ data_disk_device | regex_replace('[0-9]+$', '') }}
